    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 271102\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n302 AM PST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Expect warming temperatures and generally light breezes through \n  the weekend into the start of next week.\n\n* An incoming system will bring chances of gusty winds, widespread\n  rain, and mountain snow to the region early-to-mid week.\n\n&&\n\n\n.DISCUSSION...Today through Thursday.\n\nWeather will remain generally quiet through the region this weekend \nthanks to an area of high pressure in the southeastern Pacific \nbringing ridging to the Desert Southwest. Temperatures will continue \nto climb through the weekend - peaking on Sunday - with afternoon \nhighs ranging 8 to 10 degrees above seasonal normals. On the topic \nof warm temperatures, when it does not freeze in Las Vegas today, we \nwill tie the record longest stretch of consecutive days above \nfreezing... with a forecast to break this record on Saturday. The \ncurrent record is 674 days, which ended December 10th, 2021. The \nlast day that it froze in Las Vegas was January 22nd, 2025 with 31 \ndegrees. Winds will remain generally light across the region this \nweekend; however, we are watching the potential for gusty \nwesterly winds along the eastern slopes of the Sierra into the \nOwens Valley (20-40% chance of 40 mph gusts) and in the western \nMojave Desert (40-60% chance of 40 mph gusts) on Sunday and Sunday\nnight.\n\nUnsettled weather returns next week as a closed area of low pressure \npushes into the southern California Coast from the eastern Pacific \nOcean. This system's path continues to trend farther south, with \nensemble means now placing the center of the low between Los Angeles \nand San Diego as it moves inland. Timing has been a bit more \nconsistent in favoring Tuesday and Wednesday for impacts to our \nforecast area. Best chances for strong gusty winds exist Tuesday \nand Tuesday night across San Bernardino, Mohave, and southern \nClark counties, though chances will decrease if this system \ncontinues to trend farther south. Precipitation will push into the\nsouthern Great Basin Monday night into Tuesday morning with snow \nlevels generally between 7000 and 8000 feet before pushing \neastward through the day on Tuesday. There remains a substantial \nspread in forecast total precipitation amounts, with a 90% chance \nof 0.25 inches and a 10% chance of over 2 inches in desert \nvalleys. Again, with any additional southward shift of the main \nsystem, precipitation amounts will be subject to change. Stay \ntuned to the forecast as confidence increases through the weekend.\n&&\n\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Winds \nremain light and follow typical, directional patterns. Chance of 10+ \nknots is 10% or less throughout the TAF period. Scattered to broken \nhigh clouds, with bases at or above 15kft.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Light and diurnal wind \npatterns anticipated across most of the region today and tonight. \nThe exception is the KIFP area where northerly winds will gust 15-30 \nknots through mid-afternoon. Scattered to broken high clouds, with \nbases at or above 15kft.\n&&\n\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Soulat\nAVIATION...Woods\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
